WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.846 --> 00:00:01.949
Two steel plates,

2
00:00:02.100 --> 00:00:06.233
welded together: the weld melts their edges into one piece of steel.

3
00:00:07.244 --> 00:00:11.320
In the section: the weld metal, and beside it the heat-affected zone,

4
00:00:11.447 --> 00:00:13.665
steel that got hot but did not melt.

5
00:00:14.724 --> 00:00:18.288
A weld can look perfect outside and be wrong inside.

6
00:00:19.000 --> 00:00:20.614
So every weld is checked.

7
00:00:21.946 --> 00:00:24.651
First porosity: gas bubbles in the weld,

8
00:00:24.802 --> 00:00:27.948
usually because a draught blew the shielding gas away.

9
00:00:28.620 --> 00:00:32.266
A few small pores are often allowed; a whole row is not.

10
00:00:33.342 --> 00:00:38.056
Most feared is lack of fusion: the weld metal lies against the side wall,

11
00:00:38.137 --> 00:00:39.623
but never melted into it.

12
00:00:40.379 --> 00:00:45.290
Outside you see nothing, and under changing loads the gap grows into a crack.

13
00:00:46.024 --> 00:00:48.799
The cause: too little heat, or too much speed.

14
00:00:49.865 --> 00:00:52.651
A hydrogen crack comes hours after welding,

15
00:00:52.790 --> 00:00:56.633
in the hard steel beside the weld; moisture brings the hydrogen in.

16
00:00:57.322 --> 00:00:58.808
A crack is never allowed.

17
00:00:59.900 --> 00:01:04.509
And distortion: the weld shrinks as it cools and pulls the plates out of line.

18
00:01:05.185 --> 00:01:08.680
Clamps, pre-setting and a balanced welding sequence help.

19
00:01:10.042 --> 00:01:13.793
Dry wire and preheating, to 100 degrees for example,

20
00:01:13.909 --> 00:01:18.494
fight hydrogen: the weld cools more slowly, and the hydrogen escapes.

21
00:01:19.202 --> 00:01:23.602
On thick welds the final check often waits 16 to 48 hours.

22
00:01:24.626 --> 00:01:26.205
How big may a defect be?

23
00:01:26.916 --> 00:01:29.389
ISO 5817 says.

24
00:01:30.124 --> 00:01:31.703
Level B is the strictest,

25
00:01:31.865 --> 00:01:36.753
for cranes and pressure parts; C is normal structural work, D light work.

26
00:01:37.803 --> 00:01:41.344
A boom, with lack of fusion in three of its forty welds.

27
00:01:42.081 --> 00:01:45.077
The procedure said 300 millimetres per minute;

28
00:01:45.297 --> 00:01:49.581
they welded at 405: 35 percent too fast.

29
00:01:50.647 --> 00:01:52.841
That is 26 percent less heat.

30
00:01:53.576 --> 00:01:58.707
Each repair takes about three and a half hours: gouge out, reweld, retest.

31
00:02:00.073 --> 00:02:04.009
Checking without breaking is called NDT: non-destructive testing.

32
00:02:04.729 --> 00:02:09.082
It starts with looking: weld size, undercut, pores and cracks.

33
00:02:10.069 --> 00:02:12.089
A fine crack is often invisible.

34
00:02:12.802 --> 00:02:16.053
In dye penetrant testing a red liquid seeps into it.

35
00:02:17.139 --> 00:02:22.271
Wipe it off, spray on white developer, which draws the liquid back out: a red line.

36
00:02:22.999 --> 00:02:25.751
Quick and cheap, but only at the surface.

37
00:02:27.074 --> 00:02:32.078
For flaws inside there is ultrasonic testing: a probe sends sound pulses in,

38
00:02:32.264 --> 00:02:35.550
and a flat flaw like lack of fusion bounces them back.

39
00:02:36.245 --> 00:02:40.146
X-ray sees round pores better, but often misses flat flaws,

40
00:02:40.308 --> 00:02:42.305
and everyone must leave the area.

41
00:02:43.330 --> 00:02:48.891
With spot welds, breaking parts works too: tear open one part per robot per shift,

42
00:02:49.077 --> 00:02:53.395
and measure all 32 welds, at least 4.4 millimetres.

43
00:02:54.104 --> 00:02:58.562
With 12 robots, three shifts and 140 euros a part,

44
00:02:58.759 --> 00:03:01.836
that costs five thousand and forty euros a day.

45
00:03:02.804 --> 00:03:06.194
The WPS, the welding procedure specification,

46
00:03:06.310 --> 00:03:11.221
is the recipe at the workstation; the WPQR, the qualification record,

47
00:03:11.372 --> 00:03:13.647
proves with a tested piece that it works.

48
00:03:14.952 --> 00:03:19.898
An angle probe beside the weld sends the sound in at a slant, often at 45,

49
00:03:20.049 --> 00:03:21.883
60 or 70 degrees.

50
00:03:22.595 --> 00:03:26.531
Here the 60 degree beam meets the flawed side wall square on:

51
00:03:26.774 --> 00:03:28.609
the echo comes straight back.

52
00:03:29.690 --> 00:03:34.915
On the A-scan each peak is an echo: the further right, the longer the sound path.

53
00:03:35.612 --> 00:03:38.677
The operator compares its height with a reference block,

54
00:03:38.863 --> 00:03:40.453
and measures the flaw’s length.

55
00:03:41.748 --> 00:03:44.848
The designer or customer sets the level on the drawing.

56
00:03:45.566 --> 00:03:49.293
In structural steel the execution class gives a default,

57
00:03:49.432 --> 00:03:53.577
from EXC1 for light work to EXC4 for bridges.

58
00:03:54.306 --> 00:04:00.263
Usually EXC1 means D, EXC2 C, and EXC3 B.

59
00:04:01.236 --> 00:04:05.044
A hall in EXC2 has 160 butt welds,

60
00:04:05.242 --> 00:04:09.991
and the specification asks for 10 percent ultrasonic: 16 welds.

61
00:04:11.011 --> 00:04:13.252
Magnetic particle testing is quick,

62
00:04:13.438 --> 00:04:19.173
on fillet welds too: a yoke magnetises the steel, and iron powder gathers at cracks,

63
00:04:19.417 --> 00:04:21.112
even just below the surface.

64
00:04:21.827 --> 00:04:26.238
It only works on magnetic steel; for stainless, use penetrant.

65
00:04:27.326 --> 00:04:32.934
Whoever tests is certified to ISO 9712: level 1 works supervised,

66
00:04:33.131 --> 00:04:37.137
level 2 tests and assesses, level 3 writes the procedures.

67
00:04:37.838 --> 00:04:40.543
A test before the waiting time does not count.

68
00:04:41.933 --> 00:04:46.101
A manager watches the repair rate: metres repaired over metres tested.

69
00:04:46.844 --> 00:04:51.697
Two thousand four hundred tested, 62 repaired: 2.6 percent.

70
00:04:52.773 --> 00:04:59.739
Record every defect with a code from ISO 6520-1, such as 401 for lack of fusion,

71
00:04:59.843 --> 00:05:01.910
plus welder, position and shift.

72
00:05:02.658 --> 00:05:04.016
Put that in a Pareto.

73
00:05:05.009 --> 00:05:09.281
41 metres of lack of fusion, 12 of undercut, 9 of pores.

74
00:05:10.066 --> 00:05:15.081
And 33 of those 41 metres were vertical-up, across all welders.

75
00:05:15.788 --> 00:05:21.198
The defect follows the position, not the person: check the technique and WPS there.

76
00:05:22.522 --> 00:05:26.887
After welding, residual stress stays behind: the weld wants to shrink,

77
00:05:27.050 --> 00:05:29.268
and the cold steel holds it back.

78
00:05:30.048 --> 00:05:32.614
Machine it later, and the part warps.

79
00:05:33.620 --> 00:05:38.798
So first, stress relieving: heat slowly in a furnace, hold, and cool slowly.

80
00:05:39.828 --> 00:05:43.509
And the silent cost: overwelding, welding bigger than asked.

81
00:05:44.286 --> 00:05:46.724
Weld metal grows with the square of the throat:

82
00:05:46.887 --> 00:05:51.357
throat 5 instead of 4 costs 56 percent more wire and time,

83
00:05:51.577 --> 00:05:52.761
and adds distortion.
